# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
james-will-1.md:1: argue/readings: S5 is read 2 ways: james-will-1.logic.r1 has "past_bagged → dispense_warrant" and "past_bagged"; james-will-1.logic.r1.2 and james-will-1.logic.r1.3 have "past_bagged → dispense_warrant"; they differ when nothing is true, which the second allows
james-will-1.md:1: argue/answered: S5: answered yes, which keeps james-will-1.logic.r1.2 and james-will-1.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "we have already securely bagged our facts in a certain order" and "we can dispense with any further warrant for that order of past facts" are false. Is S5, "Truly enough, if we have {81} already securely bagged our facts in a certain order, we can dispense with any further warrant for that order.", true then?
james-will-1.md:1: argue/readings: S7 is read 2 ways: james-will-1.logic.r1 has "¬(drop_past → empty_future) ∨ ¬◇¬(drop_past → empty_future)" and "¬□(drop_past → empty_future)"; james-will-1.logic.r1.2 and james-will-1.logic.r1.3 have nothing from it; they differ when only "substance may be dropped from our conception of the irrecoverably past", "substance is an equally empty complication to our notions of the future" and "possibly, it is not so that (if substance may be dropped from our conception of the irrecoverably past, then substance is an equally empty complication to our notions of the future)" are true, which the second allows
james-will-1.md:1: argue/modal-ambiguous: S7 is read james-will-1.logic.r1.2 and james-will-1.logic.r1.3 with no modality; james-will-1.logic.r1 as what is necessary or possible: its "may" or "need" does not say which it means
james-will-1.md:1: argue/answered: S7: answered yes, which keeps james-will-1.logic.r1.2 and james-will-1.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "substance may be dropped from our conception of the irrecoverably past", "substance is an equally empty complication to our notions of the future" and "possibly, it is not so that (if substance may be dropped from our conception of the irrecoverably past, then substance is an equally empty complication to our notions of the future)" are true and "necessarily, (if substance may be dropped from our conception of the irrecoverably past, then substance is an equally empty complication to our notions of the future)" is false. Is S7, "It does not follow that if substance may be dropped from our conception of the irrecoverably past, it need be an equally empty complication to our notions of the future.", true then?
